1. Business Problem
The business problem is data science job salaries. Organization is hiring for data science roles often struggle to determine the fair and competitive salaries. Compensation varies widely by work year, experience level, employment type, job title, salary, remote ratio, etc. Without reliable benchmarking data, HR teams and hiring managers may set salaries based on guesswork or outdated information rather than current market trends. This can cause organization to overpay for unecessarily or underpay compared to competitors, risking either wasted budget or loss of talent. This problem is important to the organization because it help organization to attract and keep good data science talent. 
If pay is too low, employees may leave for better offers, and organization struggles to fill important role. If pay is too high, it wastes budget and reduce profit, especially when hiring more people in the same role. This problem also affects trust within the organization, because employees expect fair pay compare to others doing similar role.
This problem is affected by HR teams, Hiring Managers, Current Employees, Job Seekers
2. Data
Work year, Experience level, Employment type, Job title, Salary, Salary currency, Salary in usd, Employee residence, Remote ratio, Company location, Company size.
3. Features & Target
Features: Work year, Experience level, Employment type, Job title , Salary in USD, Employee residence, Remote ratio , Company location, Company size
Target: Predict how much a full time entry level data scientist is paid at USD.
4. AI/ML Approach
- Learning types: Supervised learning - The organization will use historical data showing the salaries of data science jobs. I'm using supervised learning because it is the historical data and it already have correct answer.
Would the problem involve classification or prediction?
- Task types: Prediction (Regression) - It using to predict the salary of data science's jobs.
5. Data Quality Risks
- Missing information: Some records may have blank or null values for fields like remote ratio or company size
- Inconsistent formats: Job titles, experience levels, or company location might be entered differently accross records
- Outdated information: Salary data from earlier work years may not reflect
- If the data is poor, the model will learn wrong or misleading patterns. This lowers the model's accuracy. Missing or consistent values can lead to biased prediction. Outdated salary data may cause the model to suggest pay that doesn't match today's market.
